abstract: >-
  We prove that certifying self-modifications of total cost A with at most
  theta expected false acceptances costs (A/log(1/epsilon))(H(p) + log(1/theta))
  up to an additive A, that a sound infinite self-improvement of finite cost
  exists exactly when the sum of a_n log(1/a_n) converges, and that a proposer
  needs at least exp(H_{1/2}(pi)) proposals, a bound that language models miss
  by factors of 1.5 to 4.3. A typed crossing network with shared memory, run on
  five open problems including the Yang-Mills mass gap and the Hodge
  conjecture, reaches what its libraries can reach with half the checks;
  Hebbian boosters add nothing, and none of the five problems is solved.
